# Shell scripts must reach a remote host with LF endings.
#
# `install.sh` is fetched with curl and piped straight into `sh`. A single CR at the end of the
# shebang makes that fail with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M` — on someone else's machine, in a
# one-line command with nothing to debug. Nothing here is checked in with CRLF today; this is so
# a commit from a Windows checkout with different settings cannot change that quietly.
#
# Deliberately narrow: no `* text=auto`, which would renormalize the whole repository in one
# commit and bury real changes under thousands of line-ending diffs.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The generated contracts are written by a script and read back by it.
#
# `scripts/emit-mcp-tools.mjs` writes LF and `--check` re-reads the file; with `core.autocrlf=true`
# and no rule here, every checkout and every rebase handed it CRLF, so the check answered *the MCP
# surface has changed* after a rebase that changed nothing. The comparison normalises now — that is
# the real fix and it holds however the file arrives — but a working copy that differs from what
# the generator writes also makes `git diff` noisy for a file nobody edits by hand. Same narrowness
# as the rule above: two named files, not a directory glob that would pull in whatever lands there.
contract/mcp-tools-v1.json text eol=lf
contract/broker-v1.json text eol=lf

